We have verified our entire analysis by injecting artificial HPDM signals into our data set and ensuring that the analysis correctly identified them. For example, when we added a monochromatic signal of the form in \eqref{Bhpdm} with $\varepsilon=10^{-5}$ and $m_{A'}=10^{-14}\,\mathrm{eV}$ to the time series data from each station, and re-ran our analysis, we found the resulting limit only changed in the vicinity of $m_{A'}=10^{-14}\,\mathrm{eV}$, where it became $\hat\varepsilon\sim1.4\times10^{-5}$.  (Note that the limit is slightly weaker than the injected signal, as expected.)  Moreover, the candidate analysis correctly identified DM candidates near the injected masses with high significance.  We applied a similar verification process to the axion analysis described in the next section.

